      {
        "id": "def-hermitian-residual-orthogonality-certificate",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Residual and near-orthogonality data for an approximate Hermitian eigenbasis",
        "deps": [
          "def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "statement": "Let $\\mathcal A$ be a nonempty family of Hermitian matrices in $\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}$, let $V\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}$, and let $\\Theta=\\operatorname{diag}(\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_n)$ with real $\\theta_j$. A **residual and orthogonality certificate** is a tuple of nonnegative numbers $\\delta,\\rho,M_A,M_\\Theta,\\tau$ such that, for every $A\\in\\mathcal A$,\n$$\\|V^*V-I\\|_2\\le\\delta<1,\\quad \\|AV-V\\Theta\\|_2\\le\\rho,\\quad \\|A\\|_2\\le M_A,\\quad \\|\\Theta\\|_2\\le M_\\Theta,\\quad 1-\\sqrt{1-\\delta}\\le\\tau.$$\nThe certificate uses exact inequalities or separately verified upper bounds. Its eigenvalue enclosure radius is $\\eta=\\rho+(M_A+M_\\Theta)\\tau$. This definition does not assert that computed residuals or Gram matrices satisfy the bounds; that is a separate certification procedure.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-verified-linear-residual-certificate-data",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Verified point and uniform linear residual certificate data",
        "deps": [
          "def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-brouwer-fixed-point-for-closed-boxes",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Brouwer fixed points exist on every nonempty closed box",
        "deps": [
          "thm-brouwer-fixed-point-theorem"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-complex-hermitian-realification-identities",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Realification preserves Hermitian spectra, norms, and congruence",
        "deps": [
          "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms",
          "def-self-adjoint-and-normal-endomorphism",
          "thm-rank-nullity",
          "def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space",
          "thm-matrix-of-the-adjoint-is-the-conjugate-transpose"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "statement": "For $m,n,p\\in\\mathbb N$ and $Z=X+iY\\in\\mathbb C^{m\\times n}$ with real $X,Y$, define its realification\n$$\n\\mathcal R(Z)=\\begin{pmatrix}X&-Y\\\\Y&X\\end{pmatrix}\\in\\mathbb R^{2m\\times2n}.\n$$\nUse the standard coordinate inner products $\\langle z,w\\rangle=\\sum_j z_j\\overline{w_j}$ on $\\mathbb C^k$ and $\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\sum_j x_jy_j$ on $\\mathbb R^k$, with their induced 2-norms. For compatible $Z\\in\\mathbb C^{m\\times n}$ and $W\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times p}$,\n$$\n\\mathcal R(ZW)=\\mathcal R(Z)\\mathcal R(W).\n$$\nFor any $Z\\in\\mathbb C^{m\\times n}$, $\\mathcal R(Z^*)=\\mathcal R(Z)^{\\mathsf T}$. For square $Z\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}$, $Z$ is invertible exactly when $\\mathcal R(Z)$ is invertible. If $H=H^*$ is Hermitian with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_n$ counted with multiplicity, then $\\mathcal R(H)$ is real symmetric with eigenvalue multiset $\\{\\lambda_j,\\lambda_j:1\\le j\\le n\\}$. For every complex matrix $E\\in\\mathbb C^{m\\times n}$, $\\|\\mathcal R(E)\\|_2=\\|E\\|_2$.\n\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-hermitian-eigenvalue-frobenius-bound",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Hermitian eigenvalues lie within a certified Frobenius bound",
        "deps": [
          "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms",
          "def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space",
          "thm-spectral-and-frobenius-norms-are-unitarily-invariant-with-singular-value-formulas-and-rank-comparison"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-hermitian-rayleigh-residual-spectrum-distance",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "A Hermitian approximate-eigenpair residual locates a spectral value",
        "deps": [
          "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms",
          "def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "statement": "Let $A=A^*\\in\\mathbb C^{n\\times n}$, $n\\ge1$, and equip $\\mathbb C^n$ with the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\\overline{y_j}$ and its induced norm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$. For $v\\in\\mathbb C^n\\setminus\\{0\\}$ and $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$, if $\\rho\\ge\\|Av-\\theta v\\|_2$ and $0<\\nu\\le\\|v\\|_2$, then some eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of $A$ satisfies $|\\lambda-\\theta|\\le\\rho/\\nu$.\n\n"
      },
